{"key":"t65-bio-1.1","kind":"mcq","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"A","final_answer":"Much of the heat added to water is used to break hydrogen bonds between water molecules before the molecules can move faster.","working":"Water's high specific heat is mainly due to hydrogen bonding between water molecules. When heat is added, much of the energy is used to disrupt or break these hydrogen bonds rather than immediately increasing molecular kinetic energy. Since temperature reflects average kinetic energy, more heat must be absorbed to raise water's temperature. Covalent bonds within water molecules are not broken by ordinary heating, evaporation is a cooling process but does not explain specific heat, and water's density is not the cause. Thus A is correct.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":466,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":82,"cacheReadTokens":384},"outputTokens":316,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":141,"reasoningTokens":175},"totalTokens":782,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":466,"completion_tokens":316,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":384,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":82,"total_tokens":782,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":384},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":175}},"reasoningTokens":175,"cachedInputTokens":384},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":5281,"keyed_label":"A"}
