{"key":"t65-bio-1.1","kind":"mcq","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"A","final_answer":"Much of the heat added to water is used to break hydrogen bonds between water molecules before the molecules can move faster.","working":"Specific heat is the amount of heat that must be absorbed or lost for 1 g of a substance to change its temperature by 1°C. Temperature measures the average kinetic energy (speed) of molecules. Because water molecules are extensively interconnected by hydrogen bonds, a substantial portion of the absorbed heat energy is initially used to disrupt these intermolecular hydrogen bonds rather than immediately increasing the molecular motion (kinetic energy). Consequently, more heat is required to raise the temperature of water compared to other substances of similar molecular mass, giving it a high specific heat.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":412,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":412,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":471,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":164,"reasoningTokens":307},"totalTokens":883,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":307,"promptTokenCount":412,"candidatesTokenCount":164,"totalTokenCount":883,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":412}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":164}]},"reasoningTokens":307,"cachedInputTokens":0},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":6754,"keyed_label":"A"}
