{"key":"t65-bio-x4","kind":"mcq","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"A","final_answer":"Much of the heat added to water is used to break hydrogen bonds between water molecules before the molecules can move faster.","working":"Water's unusually high specific heat is due to extensive hydrogen bonding between water molecules. Added heat must first disrupt these intermolecular hydrogen bonds, so less energy immediately increases molecular kinetic energy and temperature. Choices B, C, and D are incorrect: covalent bonds within water molecules do not break during ordinary heating; evaporation is a separate cooling process, not the cause of high specific heat; and density does not trap heat in the way described.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":466,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":82,"cacheReadTokens":384},"outputTokens":437,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":138,"reasoningTokens":299},"totalTokens":903,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":466,"completion_tokens":437,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":384,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":82,"total_tokens":903,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":384},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":299}},"reasoningTokens":299,"cachedInputTokens":384},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":5750,"keyed_label":"A"}
