{"key":"t65-bio-x4","kind":"mcq","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"A","final_answer":"Much of the heat added to water is used to break hydrogen bonds between water molecules before the molecules can move faster.","working":"Specific heat is defined as the amount of heat energy required to raise the temperature of 1 g of a substance by 1°C. Temperature is a measure of the average kinetic energy (speed) of the molecules. In water, extensive intermolecular hydrogen bonding holds molecules together. When heat is added, a substantial portion of the energy is absorbed to disrupt and break these hydrogen bonds before the molecules can gain kinetic energy and move faster. Consequently, water requires more heat energy to achieve a given temperature increase compared to other small molecules without extensive hydrogen bonding.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":412,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":412,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":482,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":165,"reasoningTokens":317},"totalTokens":894,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":317,"promptTokenCount":412,"candidatesTokenCount":165,"totalTokenCount":894,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":412}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":165}]},"reasoningTokens":317,"cachedInputTokens":0},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":4962,"keyed_label":"A"}
{"key":"t65-bio-x4","kind":"mcq","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","pass":"audit","ok":true,"object":{"keyed_choice_correct":true,"per_choice":[{"label":"A","rationale_accurate":true,"issue":""},{"label":"B","rationale_accurate":true,"issue":""},{"label":"C","rationale_accurate":true,"issue":""},{"label":"D","rationale_accurate":false,"issue":"The rationale falsely states that 'water is densest as a solid, which is why ice sinks to the bottom of lakes in winter.' In fact, water is densest as a liquid at approximately 4 °C; solid water (ice) is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats at the top of lakes rather than sinking."}],"other_defects":[]},"usage":{"inputTokens":763,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":763,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":966,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":197,"reasoningTokens":769},"totalTokens":1729,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":769,"promptTokenCount":763,"candidatesTokenCount":197,"totalTokenCount":1729,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":763}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":197}]},"reasoningTokens":769,"cachedInputTokens":0},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":7905,"keyed_label":"A"}
{"key":"t65-bio-2.5","kind":"mcq","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"B","final_answer":"X entered by active transport, because it moved from lower to higher concentration.","working":"The outside concentration of X is 5 mM, while the inside concentration is initially 20 mM and rises to 30 mM. Therefore, if X entered the cell, it moved from a region of lower concentration to a region of higher concentration. Passive transport, whether simple diffusion or facilitated diffusion, moves substances down their concentration gradient, so choices A and C are not supported. Movement against a concentration gradient requires energy input, which is active transport. Choice D is not needed: the gradient direction is sufficient to distinguish active from passive transport.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":481,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":225,"cacheReadTokens":256},"outputTokens":6131,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":142,"reasoningTokens":5989},"totalTokens":6612,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":481,"completion_tokens":6131,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":256,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":225,"total_tokens":6612,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":256},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5989}},"reasoningTokens":5989,"cachedInputTokens":256},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":77861,"keyed_label":"B"}
